# Edge-Spectral Supersaturation for Triangles

Every network hides a soundtrack in the eigenvalues of its adjacency matrix.
This package shows how the loudest note &mdash; the spectral radius &lambda; &mdash;
forces triangles to appear once it overshoots the edge count.

## The threshold and the overshoot

Nosal's theorem says a triangle-free graph obeys $\lambda^2 \le m$. Once the
excess $q = \lambda^2 - m$ turns positive, triangles are unavoidable. Explore the
mechanism directly:

{{interactive_demo:0}}

## The one inequality behind everything

The whole method rests on a single pointwise fact: if $|\mu| \le \lambda$ then
$\mu^3 \ge -\lambda\mu^2$. Drag an eigenvalue and watch the cube stay above
its floor:

{{interactive_demo:2}}

Formally, summing this bound and keeping the top eigenvalue yields
$2\lambda^3 - \lambda\sum\mu_i^2 \le \sum\mu_i^3$, which becomes
$\lambda q \le 3t$. The cubic-domination picture:

{{visualization:0}}

